<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gautama Buddha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gautama Buddha was the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, who lived in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> around the fifth century BCE. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> note that Greek kingdoms in the east produced Gandharan art, in which Greek sculptural iconography was adapted to represent the Buddha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhisattva">bodhisattvas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha.jpg" alt="Gautama Buddha" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: พระมหาเทวประภาส วชิรญาณเมธี (ผู้ถ่าย-ปล่อยสัญญาอนุญาตภาพให้นำไปใช้ได้เพื่อการศึกษาโดยอยู่ภา่ยใต้ cc-by-sa-3.0) … · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buddha_in_Sarnath_Museum_(Dhammajak_Mutra).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Siddhartha Gautama was a wandering religious teacher of the eastern Indo-Gangetic Plains who lived in the 6th or 5th century BCE. Buddhist legends place his birth at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, to royal parents of the Shakya clan. Most scholars accept that he lived, taught and founded a monastic order during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara of Magadha</a> and died in the reign of Bimbisara's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a>, making him a contemporary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>, the Jain tirthankara.</p><p>His dates are uncertain. The long chronology of the Sri Lankan chronicles gives 624 to 544 BCE, while most scholars who accept it but date <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s coronation to about 268 BCE put his life at 566 to 486 BCE. The oldest surviving written mention of the Buddha is from the middle of the 3rd century BCE, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edicts-of-ashoka">Edicts of Ashoka</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a> pillar inscription commemorates the emperor's pilgrimage to the birthplace. The earliest biographies already contain supernatural elements, and the scholar Rupert Gethin holds that the life story is not history, nor meant to be.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>According to Buddhist tradition, he renounced home life to live as a wandering ascetic and attained nirvana at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a> in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>. He then taught across the lower Indo-Gangetic Plain and built a monastic order, the sangha. Tradition describes a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-way">Middle Way</a> between sensual indulgence and severe asceticism, summarised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-noble-truths">Four Noble Truths</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noble-eightfold-path">Noble Eightfold Path</a>. The earliest biographical material in the Pali Nikayas describes a forty-five-year career as a teacher.</p><p>He left no writings. His teachings were compiled by the Buddhist community as the Vinaya Pitaka, on monastic discipline, and the Sutra Pitaka, discourses attributed to him, passed down orally in Middle Indo-Aryan dialects. How far later doctrine reflects his own words is disputed. Scholars such as Richard Gombrich and A. K. Warder hold that early texts contain material traceable to him, while others doubt that the beliefs of pre-sectarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> can be recovered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Buddhist tradition holds that he died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushinagar">Kushinagar</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parinirvana">parinirvana</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> later developed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vajrayana">Vajrayana</a> traditions and spread beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. It declined in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and largely disappeared there after the 8th century CE, but became more prominent in Southeast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. Mahayana sutras present Shakyamuni as a transcendent being, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> tradition incorporated the Buddha as the ninth avatar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>.</p><p>The earliest artistic depictions, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharhut">Bharhut</a> and Sanchi, are aniconic, showing an empty throne, footprints, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> wheel or a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhi-tree">Bodhi tree</a>. Images in human form became widespread after the first century CE, and those of Gandharan and Central Asian Buddhism were influenced by Hellenistic art, a style known as Greco-Buddhist art.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Buddha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Siddhartha Gautama</b>, the Buddha, attained enlightenment at Bodh Gaya in Magadha and preached at Rajagriha, where Bimbisara gave his order the Veluvana bamboo grove; Vardhamana Mahavira, the great teacher of Jainism, also taught in the region, and the Ajivikas of Makkhali Gosala were active there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>By tradition the <b>Buddha</b> attained awakening at Bodh Gaya, and by Jain tradition Mahavira was born near Vaishali; both taught in the kingdoms of the Ganges plain.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sutta Nipata tells of the Brahmin teacher Bavari, who lived on the Godavari in the Assaka country and sent his pupils north to meet the <b>Buddha</b>, which shows the kingdom's links along the southern trade route, the Dakshinapatha, to the Ganges valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/assaka">Assaka</a></li><li><blockquote>Champa was counted among the six great cities of the <b>Buddha</b>'s day.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-anga">Kingdom of Anga</a></li><li><blockquote>Chanda Pradyota Mahasena, a contemporary of the <b>Buddha</b>, was remembered as violent and ambitious; he threatened Rajagriha and fought Udayana of Vatsa, who eloped with his daughter Vasavadatta.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-avanti">Kingdom of Avanti</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Buddha</b> gave his first sermon at nearby Sarnath.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kashi">Kingdom of Kashi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushinagar">Kushinagar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.48,83.28" aria-label="Lumbini on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6738 1156 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gautama Buddha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7188 1426h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7188 1426h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27.48,83.28">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For hatred does not cease by hatred at any time: hatred ceases by love, this is an old rule.&#8221;</p><footer>Dhammapada, verse 5 &#183; tr. F. Max Müller, 1881 &#183; a verse of the Dhammapada, which Buddhist tradition gives as the Buddha’s words &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2017/pg2017-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If one man conquer in battle a thousand times thousand men, and if another conquer himself, he is the greatest of conquerors.&#8221;</p><footer>Dhammapada, verse 103 &#183; tr. F. Max Müller, 1881 &#183; a verse of the Dhammapada, which Buddhist tradition gives as the Buddha’s words &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2017/pg2017-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;By oneself the evil is done, by oneself one suffers; by oneself evil is left undone, by oneself one is purified. Purity and impurity belong to oneself, no one can purify another.&#8221;</p><footer>Dhammapada, verse 165 &#183; tr. F. Max Müller, 1881 &#183; a verse of the Dhammapada, which Buddhist tradition gives as the Buddha’s words &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2017/pg2017-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
